<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Diocese of Hispania</h1><p class="st-meta">314 CE – 409 CE (approx.) · capital Emerita Augusta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The late Roman administrative unit that grouped the provinces of Iberia and north-west Africa under a single vicar based at Mérida.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The diocese was created in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian's</a> reforms around 297 to 314, grouping the provinces of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-baetica">Baetica</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/carthaginiensis">Carthaginiensis</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gallaecia">Gallaecia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Tarraconensis</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mauretania-tingitana">Mauretania Tingitana</a> across the Strait. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/balearica">Balearica</a> province was added around 369. It formed part of the Praetorian Prefecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gauls</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A vicarius at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a> oversaw the provincial governors, mainly in justice and taxation, while separate officers commanded the army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> was largely peaceful in the 4th century, a land of rich villas and cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> grew strong, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-elvira">Council of Elvira</a> shows, and the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coca-spain">Cauca</a> in the north.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 409 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alans">Alans</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> during the usurpation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-iii-western-roman-emperor">Constantine III</a> and his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerontius-general">Gerontius</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> partly restored control with Visigothic help, but the diocese effectively broke up as barbarian kingdoms took over.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/diocese-of-hispania.jpg" alt="Diocese of Hispania" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Tomisti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dioecesis_Hispaniae_locator_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>314 CE – 409 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Emerita Augusta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>, <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dioecesis Hispaniarum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=362&amp;focus=diocese-of-hispania" aria-label="Diocese of Hispania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4583 761 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Diocese of Hispania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4903 1017h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4903 1017h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Spain. <a href="/atlas?year=362&amp;focus=diocese-of-hispania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

